<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jim Crow laws</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1870s–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jim Crow laws were state and local laws enforcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a> in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from the late nineteenth century until the 1960s. Upheld by the Supreme Court in Plessy v. Ferguson (1896), they segregated schools, transport and public places and went with measures that disenfranchised Black voters, until they were struck down by court rulings and the civil rights laws of 1964 and 1965.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws.jpg" alt="Jim Crow laws" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Wilson · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_and_colored_water_fountain_then_and_now_-_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
